  "summary": "The Richmond, CA city council voted 4-3 to reinstate its Flock ALPR contract through December 2026 after the cameras were taken offline in November 2025 when Chief Simmons discovered a national search feature had made Richmond's data accessible to outside agencies. Council members debated data-sharing risks, sanctuary-city concerns, crime statistics, and allegations that a pro-Flock advocate was paid by the company.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Richmond's Flock license plate reader system will be back online for seven months, a split city council decided late Tuesday night.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "In November 2025 Richmond police Chief Timothy Simmons turned off the Automated License Plate Reader (ALPR) after finding out that a national search feature made Richmond's data accessible to outside agencies",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    },
    {
      "quote": "I find it offensive that Flock allowed that national search feature to be deployed and did not inform us that that was happening knowing that we are a sanctuary city",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock is not paying that man",
      "paragraph_idx": 47
    }
  ],
